INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - JANITOR'S CLOSET OFF THE ATRIUM - NIGHT

Mops, buckets, a bare bulb. A cracked radio plays low jazz. Shelving crowded with floor wax and light bulbs.

A WOMAN in a good wool suit sits on an overturned bucket. She has the look of someone who has been crying and has stopped. A leather briefcase rests on her knees.

At the door, an old man in coveralls leans on a push broom. He is the night custodian, MORDECAI TILLMAN (70s, silver stubble, a ring of keys the size of a dinner plate).

MORDECAI
Counselor, I don't mean to pry.

WOMAN
Then don't.

MORDECAI
But it's a quarter to midnight, and you're in my closet.

WOMAN
I needed a door that locked.

MORDECAI
Mine does. From the outside.

WOMAN
I noticed.

He sets down the broom. Pulls up a second bucket, upside down, and lowers himself onto it with a grunt.

MORDECAI
I've worked this building forty-one years. I know the sound a person makes when they've lost.

WOMAN
I haven't lost.

MORDECAI
No?

WOMAN
The verdict comes tomorrow.

MORDECAI
Then why's your face look like that?

WOMAN
Because I know how it ends.

MORDECAI
Do you now.

WOMAN
(quietly)
My client did it, Mr. Tillman.

MORDECAI
I thought so.

WOMAN
You thought so?

MORDECAI
I clean the defendants' table. They leave things behind.

She laughs, a sharp breath through her nose.

WOMAN
And what do they leave?

MORDECAI
Gum. Rosaries. Once, a gun, but that's another story.

WOMAN
(wiping her eyes)
My name is Esperanza Calloway-Reyes. Not Counselor.

MORDECAI
Pleased to meet you, Ms. Calloway-Reyes.

ESPERANZA
I'm supposed to believe in him. That's the job.

MORDECAI
And?

ESPERANZA
I did believe in him. Right up until the jury was seated.

MORDECAI
Seems a late time to stop.

ESPERANZA
It's the time I started noticing.

Silence. The radio shifts to a slow saxophone. Mordecai reaches over, turns it down.

MORDECAI
Ms. Calloway-Reyes, you want my advice?

ESPERANZA
Do I have a choice?

MORDECAI
No. But here it is. Go home. Sleep. Tomorrow, stand up and do the work.

ESPERANZA
Even if I know he's guilty?

MORDECAI
Especially then.

She nods, slowly. Stands. Tucks the briefcase under her arm.

ESPERANZA
Thank you.

MORDECAI
Don't mention it. I don't know anything.

He opens the door. She steps into the dark atrium, her heels echoing across the marble.
